%I A398429 #4 Aug 11 2026 19:45:24
%S A398429 1,2,3,5,6,8,11,15,20,25,30,39,45,55,66,83
%N A398429 a(n) is the maximum cardinality of a Sidon subset of the set of distinct multinomial coefficient values of order n.
%C A398429 Let B(n) be the set of distinct values n!/(p_1!*...*p_t!), where (p_1, ..., p_t) ranges over the partitions of n. Then a(n) is the maximum cardinality of a subset S of B(n) for which all sums x + y with x, y in S and x <= y are distinct.
%C A398429 The cardinality of B(n) is A070289(n).
%C A398429 If r + m = n, then binomial(n, r)*B(r)*B(m) is a subset of B(n).
%C A398429 The sequence a(n) - n is nondecreasing. More generally, a(n) >= a(r) + n - r for 2 <= r <= n. In particular, a(n) >= n + 67 for n >= 16.
%C A398429 The Sidon defect A070289(n) - a(n) is nondecreasing.
%C A398429 The set B(n) is Sidon if and only if 1 <= n <= 4. For n = 5, the collision 10 + 30 = 20 + 20 occurs, and the embedding transfers a collision to every order n >= 5.
%C A398429 Let H_n be the hypergraph on B(n) whose hyperedges are the supports of nontrivial equalities x + y = u + v. Then a(n) is the independence number of H_n, or equivalently A070289(n) - a(n) is its transversal number.
%C A398429 The exact values for 1 <= n <= 16 were obtained by an exhaustive SAT computation. A separate computation found a Sidon subset of B(17) having 89 elements, so a(17) >= 89; optimality has not been proved.
%C A398429 The asymptotic lower bound liminf_{n -> infinity} log(a(n)/n)*log(log(n))/sqrt(log(n)) >= Pi/sqrt(3) holds. In particular, a(n) = Omega(n*log(n)), and a(n)/n grows faster than every fixed power of log(n).
%C A398429 In the opposite direction, liminf_{n -> infinity} (A070289(n) - a(n))/(n^(3/2)*sqrt(log(n))) >= 2/(3*sqrt(3)).
%H A398429 Felix Huber, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02667">Large Sidon Subsets of Distinct Multinomial Coefficients</a>, arXiv:2608.02667 [math.CO], 2026.
%H A398429 Felix Huber, <a href="/A398429/a398429.txt">Maple program</a>
%F A398429 a(n + 1) >= a(n) + 1 for n >= 2.
%F A398429 1 <= a(n + 1) - a(n) <= A070289(n + 1) - A070289(n) for n >= 2.
%F A398429 a(r) + n - r <= a(n) <= a(r) + A070289(n) - A070289(r) for 2 <= r <= n.
%F A398429 a(n) = A070289(n) for 1 <= n <= 4, and a(n) < A070289(n) for n >= 5.
%F A398429 a(n) <= floor((sqrt(1 + 8*(binomial(A070289(n) + 1, 2) - A398233(n))) - 1)/2).
%F A398429 liminf_{n -> infinity} log(a(n)/n)*log(log(n))/sqrt(log(n)) >= Pi/sqrt(3).
%F A398429 liminf_{n -> infinity} (A070289(n) - a(n))/(n^(3/2)*sqrt(log(n))) >= 2/(3*sqrt(3)).
%e A398429 B(5) = {1, 5, 10, 20, 30, 60, 120}. This set is not Sidon because 10 + 30 = 20 + 20. The subset {1, 5, 20, 30, 60, 120} is Sidon. Since the only 7-element subset of B(5) is B(5) itself, no Sidon subset has 7 elements. Hence a(5) = 6.
%p A398429 # See Huber link
